prompt:
Implement a 3D flight simulator using Three.js. The player controls an aircraft flying over procedurally generated terrain and experiences realistic aerodynamic effects.

[Three.js Import Method (Must Be Followed Strictly)]
Use the following importmap in <head>, and import via ES Modules in <script type="module">:
<script type="importmap">
{ "imports": {
    "three": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js",
    "three/addons/": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/examples/jsm/"
} }
</script>
<script type="module">
import * as THREE from 'three';
// Write your code here
</script>

[Aircraft Model]
(1) Construct the aircraft by combining geometries in a Group:
  - Fuselage: CylinderGeometry (radius 0.5, length 6), oriented along the Z-axis, white;
  - Wings: BoxGeometry (wingspan 10 × thickness 0.15 × chord length 2), mounted on both sides of the middle of the fuselage, gray;
  - Horizontal stabilizer: BoxGeometry (span 3 × thickness 0.1 × chord length 1), mounted at the rear of the fuselage, gray;
  - Vertical stabilizer: BoxGeometry (height 1.5 × thickness 0.1 × chord length 1), mounted on top of the tail;
(2) The entire aircraft must receive shadows;

[Flight Physics]
(3) Use a simplified aerodynamic model:
  - Lift: L = 0.5 * Cl * rho * v² * S
    where Cl is the lift coefficient (based on angle of attack, simplified as Cl = 2π × alpha, where alpha is the angle of attack in radians, with an upper limit of Cl_max = 1.5)
    rho is air density, v is airspeed, and S is the wing area (approximately 20 m²)
  - Drag: D = 0.5 * Cd * rho * v² * S
    Cd = 0.02 + Cl² / (π × AR × e), AR = wingspan²/S = 5, e = 0.8 (Oswald efficiency factor)
  - Thrust: T = throttle × maxThrust (maxThrust = 5000 N)
  - Weight: W = mass × g (mass = 1000 kg, g = 9.8 m/s²)
(4) Air density varies with altitude: rho = 1.225 × exp(-altitude / 8500) (a simplified International Standard Atmosphere model);
(5) Stall: when the airspeed is below the stall speed (approximately 25 m/s at sea level), lift drops sharply and the aircraft begins to descend. Stall speed increases with altitude (due to reduced air density);

[Flight Controls]
(6) W key: push the stick forward (pitch down, nose downward, increasing the angle of attack);
(7) S key: pull the stick back (pitch up, nose upward, decreasing the angle of attack);
(8) A key: roll left;
(9) D key: roll right;
(10) Q key: yaw left;
(11) E key: yaw right;
(12) Shift key: increase throttle (throttle += 0.02 per frame, maximum 1.0);
(13) Ctrl key (or Z key): decrease throttle (throttle -= 0.02 per frame, minimum 0.0);
(14) Control rotation rates: pitch 2 rad/s, roll 3 rad/s, yaw 1 rad/s;

[Camera]
(15) Third-person follow camera: positioned behind and above the aircraft (offset approximately (0, 3, -15)), always looking toward the front of the aircraft;
(16) The camera must follow smoothly (lerp, coefficient 0.05-0.1);

[Terrain]
(17) Procedural terrain: use PlaneGeometry (200×200 with 100×100 segments), and generate a heightmap by combining simple sine waves;
(18) Terrain height range: 0-50 units;
(19) Use a green/brown gradient material for the terrain;

[Instrument Panel HUD]
(20) Display flight instruments in the interface:
  - Airspeed (id="airspeed"): unit m/s
  - Altitude (id="altitude"): unit m
  - Throttle (id="throttle"): percentage 0-100%
  - Heading (id="heading"): 0-360°
  - Vertical speed (id="vsi"): unit m/s, positive = climbing, negative = descending
  - Stall warning: when airspeed < stall speed, the text "STALL" must flash on the HUD (id="stallWarning")

[State Exposure]
(21) Update window.__3D_STATE__ every frame with:
  - position: { x, y, z } (aircraft position, y = altitude)
  - rotation: { pitch, yaw, roll } (aircraft attitude, in radians)
  - airspeed: number (airspeed in m/s)
  - altitude: number (altitude in m)
  - throttle: number (throttle 0-1)
  - heading: number (heading angle 0-360°)
  - verticalSpeed: number (vertical speed in m/s)
  - isStalling: boolean (whether the aircraft is currently stalled)
  - liftForce: number (current lift force in N)

Output a single HTML file that can be run by opening it directly in a browser.
